    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "The flow behavior of emulsions through tubes as well as both consolidated and unconsolidated synthetic porous media is described analytically and verified experimentally.A correlation technique was developed for the unconsolidated porous medium by coupling the rheological properties of the emulsions, determined from classical viscometric experiments, with the results from porous medium experiments, utilizing the \"hydraulic-radius concept\" and the Blake-Kozeny-Carman equation. For the consolidated porous medium, the \"generalized scale-up\" method was adapted to describe the gradient dependence of the viscosities of the non-Newtonian emulsions within the porous matrix. Rheological characterization was carried out with a set of six capillary rheometers and particle size and particle size distribution were determined using a Model B Industrial Coulter Counter.Emulsion qualities (volume per cent of Soltrol in the emulsion) up to and including the 50 per cent quality were found to be Newtonian for the entire range of shear rate and shear stress investigated, - 1 - 1 2 2(10 sec to 15,000 sec and 10 dynes/cm to 2500 dynes/cm, respectively). The 60 and 70 per cent qualities were characterized by Newtonian behavior at low shear values and non-Newtonian pseudoplastic behavior after some critical shear rates and shear stresses, both of which depend on emulsion quality. Fluid slippage at the capillary walls was non-existent, and the rheological characteristics were found to be independent of capillary dimensions.The emulsion volume diameters ranged from 0.48 micron to 0.718 micron, and were found to follow a log-normal distribution with a narrow range of geometric standard deviation. The volume median diameter, arithmetic mean diameter, geometric mean diameter, geometric standard deviation, polydispersity and oil-water interfacial area, were all correlated with emulsion quality.The theoretical model for the unconsolidated porous medium was tested by comparing the experimental and predicted friction factor- Reynolds number relationship. With forty experimental points, errors in friction factors were found to be 14.6 per cent and 17.4 per cent for the 60 and 70 per cent emulsion qualities, respectively. For the consolidated porous medium, the correlation method was tested by comparing the predicted \"filter\" velocities with those obtained experimentally. The errors, calculated with forty experimental points, were 9.3 per cent for the 60 per cent emulsion quality, and 13.8 per cent for the 70 per cent quality. For all porous media, the results conclusively showed that surface effects, as well as viscoelastic or normal stress effects were absent, and, in general, there was no discrimination between the rheological results from porous media and those from the classical capillary viscometric experiments in the instance of these emulsions.",
